1
00:00:01,197 --> 00:00:10,387
This briefing covers three things. Why governed AI delivery matters now. How the platform works. And how it compares with the alternatives.

2
00:00:11,191 --> 00:00:20,317
AI coding tools are now close to universal. In Google's DORA survey, ninety percent of developers use AI at work. But trust has not kept up.

3
00:00:20,317 --> 00:00:24,684
Fewer than seven percent would trust AI with an important decision.

4
00:00:24,684 --> 00:00:32,441
In the United Kingdom, eighty percent of financial firms run AI agents, but only two percent allow them broad autonomy.

5
00:00:33,246 --> 00:00:37,759
Each developer produces more, but delivery as a whole is under strain.

6
00:00:37,759 --> 00:00:43,820
One vendor study of about twenty-two thousand developers reports a third more tasks completed.

7
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:51,686
It also reports more bugs, more incidents, and review times more than five times longer. Review has become the bottleneck.

8
00:00:52,491 --> 00:01:01,011
On a five-level scale, most regions sit between assisted coding and task agents. This is our estimate from public evidence.

9
00:01:01,011 --> 00:01:08,076
Canada sits at about level one and a half. No bank, in any region, has publicly described level three:

10
00:01:08,076 --> 00:01:14,171
governed delivery with named approvers at every stage. That is where this platform aims.

11
00:01:14,976 --> 00:01:27,003
Canadian banks are strong on AI overall. RBC ranks third of fifty banks on the 2026 Evident AI Index, and TD is in the top ten.

12
00:01:27,003 --> 00:01:36,378
The regulator is explicit too. OSFI published a bulletin on generative and agentic AI in July 2026.

13
00:01:36,378 --> 00:01:41,776
Its model risk guideline, E-23, takes effect in May 2027.

14
00:01:42,579 --> 00:01:50,504
The platform answers that gap. A business person describes a change. An AI agent carries it through eight stages.

15
00:01:50,504 --> 00:01:58,779
A named person approves each gate, and every decision lands in one evidence trail. Developers keep working in VS Code.

16
00:02:00,894 --> 00:02:08,074
This is the working prototype. Everything you see is clickable, with sample data. Let's follow one working day.

17
00:02:08,875 --> 00:02:17,195
Alex is a solution architect. His home page shows only the decisions waiting for him, oldest first, and says exactly what each one is.

18
00:02:17,995 --> 00:02:24,671
The agent has written an architecture decision. Alex can read a plain summary, or switch to the full technical version.

19
00:02:24,671 --> 00:02:26,915
Every statement is linked to its source.

20
00:02:27,718 --> 00:02:36,994
How the agent did this shows its confidence and why, what it is unsure about, the sources and checks it used, each step it took, and its time and cost.

21
00:02:36,994 --> 00:02:40,188
Alex can ask the agent a question before he decides.

22
00:02:40,990 --> 00:02:50,100
Alex accepts the one gap the agent called out, and approves. Each decision can be undone for a short time, and is recorded in the audit log.

23
00:02:50,901 --> 00:02:59,067
The run page shows where the work is. The plan is split into stories, and each story is specified, designed and built one at a time.

24
00:02:59,067 --> 00:03:08,161
Any document opens in place, with where it is stored: the file in Azure Blob Storage, its versions and approvals in Azure SQL, and its fingerprint.

25
00:03:08,964 --> 00:03:13,807
Building happens on the developer's machine. The platform prepares the assignment.

26
00:03:13,807 --> 00:03:24,614
The developer copies the prompt into the standalone Developer Agent in VS Code, and the platform tracks the branch, the Azure Boards task, the pull request, and the completion report.

27
00:03:25,416 --> 00:03:30,569
When the agent is unsure what the business meant, it asks the person who made the request.

28
00:03:30,569 --> 00:03:35,836
Priya, the product owner, picks an answer, and the agent goes on to write the specification.

29
00:03:36,637 --> 00:03:45,397
Jon, the engineering lead, is on leave, so Alex reviews the code as his named backup. He opens a changed file, and approves the build.

30
00:03:46,197 --> 00:03:55,454
After the build is approved, integration runs. Tom, the integration lead, sees the pipeline results and approves, or sends the work back with a note.

31
00:03:55,454 --> 00:03:58,747
Mara, the quality lead, does the same for validation.

32
00:04:05,648 --> 00:04:14,803
Anyone with the right role can start a new request in plain language. The agent drafts a delivery plan in seconds, and the product owner approves it.

33
00:04:14,803 --> 00:04:17,568
A requester can never approve their own plan.

34
00:04:18,369 --> 00:04:26,009
Sam, the release manager, checks that validation and acceptance testing are done, picks a release window, and schedules the release.

35
00:04:26,009 --> 00:04:30,639
Until it goes live it can be cancelled, and after release it can be rolled back.

36
00:04:31,439 --> 00:04:40,498
Dana, the platform administrator, sees everything that needs attention: approvers on leave, stuck agents, change freezes, and incomplete squads.

37
00:04:40,498 --> 00:04:42,259
She fixes each one in place.

38
00:04:43,666 --> 00:04:49,436
She invites people, sets up cover while someone is away, and decides what each role is allowed to do.

39
00:04:50,237 --> 00:04:58,697
Every action, by a person or by the agent, lands in an audit log with a tamper-evident chain, and can be exported for auditors.

40
00:04:59,497 --> 00:05:01,757
The whole product also works in dark mode.

41
00:05:02,558 --> 00:05:09,131
How does this compare with the alternatives? We scored three ways of working for nine roles. This platform.

42
00:05:09,131 --> 00:05:17,669
Spec-driven tools such as Spec Kit, which keep specifications in the code repository. And working only in the code editor with an AI agent.

43
00:05:17,669 --> 00:05:24,181
The platform scores highest for the product owner, the release manager, the administrator and the auditor.

44
00:05:24,181 --> 00:05:33,088
Developers score higher with the tools that live in their editor. These scores are our own assessment, and the platform is scored as a prototype.

45
00:05:33,896 --> 00:05:41,625
We walked one small change through all three. In a regulated organisation, the platform needs three tools instead of six.

46
00:05:41,625 --> 00:05:50,630
It records seven approvals instead of two, three of them before any code is written. It also adds more waits for a person, eight against six.

47
00:05:50,630 --> 00:05:54,846
That is why a fast path for low-risk changes is the next priority.

48
00:05:55,647 --> 00:06:01,709
So the fit is clear. For small teams and developer-only changes, the editor tools are enough.

49
00:06:01,709 --> 00:06:09,074
For regulated, audited change that involves several roles, the platform is the better fit. And they combine well:

50
00:06:09,074 --> 00:06:13,637
the platform governs, and the developer's agent connects from VS Code.

51
00:06:15,436 --> 00:06:23,965
One governed flow, from business request to release. Named people accountable at every gate, and one evidence trail an auditor can follow.

52
00:06:23,965 --> 00:06:25,386
Thank you for watching.
